  {
    t: 'P25 · Union-Find (Disjoint Set Union)',
    learn: `<div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">The idea</div>
<p class="learn-p">Keep a forest where each set is a tree and every node points at its parent. <b>find(x)</b> walks to the root - the set's identity. <b>union(a,b)</b> points one root at the other. With <b>path compression</b> (flatten on the way back) and <b>union by rank</b> (hang the shorter tree under the taller), both operations are effectively O(1).</p>
</div>

<div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Spot it when you see</div>
<ul class="learn-list">
<li>"are these connected", "how many groups", <b>edges arriving one at a time</b></li>
<li>"redundant connection", "valid tree", "accounts merge"</li>
<li>Kruskal's MST - the cycle check is exactly a DSU query</li>
</ul>
<div class="learn-tip"><b>The move:</b> <code>if (find(a) == find(b)) this edge makes a cycle; else union and decrement the component count.</code> Use DSU when edges stream in; use DFS/BFS when the whole graph is known up front.</div>
</div>

<div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Picture: path compression flattens the tree</div>
<div class="learn-diagram">union(1,2)  union(3,4)  union(2,4)

  after unions            <span class="dg-k">find(4)</span> walks 4 -&gt; 3 -&gt; 1
      1                   and REWIRES everything it passed
     / \\
    2   3        before:      1          after:      1
         \\                   / \\                  / | \\
          4                 2   3                2  3  4
                                 \\
                                  4          <span class="dg-ok">tree is now flat</span>

<span class="dg-k">Union by rank</span> - always hang the SHORTER tree under the taller

   bad (no rank)            good (by rank)
     1                          1
      \\                        /|\\
       2                      2  3  4
        \\
         3        depth grows      depth stays 2
          \\       to O(n)
           4

<span class="dg-c">Together the two optimisations give near-constant
amortised time: O(alpha(n)), where alpha is the inverse
Ackermann function and is under 5 for any real input.</span>

<span class="dg-k">Cycle detection</span>   edge (u,v)
   find(u) == find(v)  -&gt;  already connected  -&gt;  <span class="dg-no">cycle</span>
   otherwise           -&gt;  union them         -&gt;  <span class="dg-ok">components -= 1</span></div>
</div>

<div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Solved: 684. Redundant Connection</div>
<p class="learn-p"><b>in</b> <code>edges = [[1,2],[1,3],[2,3]]</code> &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; <b>out</b> <code>[2,3]</code></p>
<table class="learn-table">
<tr><th>Edge</th><th>find(u)</th><th>find(v)</th><th>Same?</th><th>Action</th></tr>
<tr><td>[1,2]</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>no</td><td>union &rarr; {1,2}</td></tr>
<tr><td>[1,3]</td><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>no</td><td>union &rarr; {1,2,3}</td></tr>
<tr><td>[2,3]</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td><b>yes</b></td><td><b>cycle - this is the answer</b></td></tr>
</table>
<p class="learn-p"><b>Why DSU rather than DFS here:</b> the question asks for the <b>last</b> edge that creates a cycle, and edges arrive in order. DSU processes them incrementally and answers the moment the cycle appears. DFS would need to re-examine the graph after each insertion.</p>
</div>

<div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Rest of the pattern</div>
<table class="learn-table">
<tr><th>#</th><th>Problem</th><th>What changes</th></tr>
<tr><td>200</td><td>Number of Islands</td><td>Union adjacent land cells; the answer is the component count. DFS is simpler here, but DSU is required for the streaming version below.</td></tr>
<tr><td>305</td><td>Number of Islands II</td><td>Land appears <b>one cell at a time</b> - DFS would be O(k&middot;mn). DSU updates incrementally in near O(1) per addition.</td></tr>
<tr><td>261</td><td>Graph Valid Tree</td><td>A tree needs exactly <code>n-1</code> edges <b>and</b> no cycle. Check both - either alone is insufficient.</td></tr>
<tr><td>323</td><td>Connected Components</td><td>Start the count at n and decrement on every successful union.</td></tr>
<tr><td>721</td><td>Accounts Merge</td><td>Union emails that share an account, then group by root. The classic "merge entities by shared attribute".</td></tr>
<tr><td>990</td><td>Satisfiability of Equality Equations</td><td>Process all <code>==</code> first to build the sets, then verify no <code>!=</code> connects two members of the same set.</td></tr>
<tr><td>1584</td><td>Min Cost to Connect All Points</td><td>Kruskal's MST - sort edges, union while they do not form a cycle.</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="learn-warn"><b>Trap:</b> always union the <b>roots</b>, never the raw nodes. <code>parent[a] = b</code> corrupts the structure; it must be <code>parent[find(a)] = find(b)</code>. This is the most common DSU bug and it produces silently wrong component counts.</div>
</div>

<div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Why this pattern matters</div>
<p class="learn-p">DSU is the right structure whenever connectivity is <b>built up incrementally</b> - network links coming online, friendships forming, pixels being merged in image segmentation. Its defining strength is that it answers "same group?" in near-constant time without ever re-traversing the graph.</p>
<p class="learn-p">It is also the engine inside Kruskal's algorithm, so understanding it gives you MST for free.</p>
</div>

<div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Core concepts</div>
<ul class="learn-list">
<li><b>Representative element.</b> Each set is identified by its root, so equality of roots means equality of sets.</li>
<li><b>Path compression.</b> Rewire every node on the find path straight to the root.</li>
<li><b>Union by rank / size.</b> Keeps trees shallow by attaching the smaller under the larger.</li>
<li><b>No splitting.</b> DSU supports merge and query, but not deletion - if you need removal, process edges in reverse.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Complexity</div>
<table class="learn-table">
<tr><th>Operation</th><th>Time</th></tr>
<tr><td>find / union, both optimisations</td><td>O(&alpha;(n)) - effectively O(1)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Path compression only</td><td>O(log n) amortised</td></tr>
<tr><td>Neither optimisation</td><td>O(n) worst case</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kruskal's MST</td><td>O(E log E) - sorting dominates</td></tr>
</table>
<p class="learn-p">&alpha; is the inverse Ackermann function; it is below 5 for any input that fits in the universe, so DSU is treated as constant time in practice.</p>
</div>

<div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Interview Spotlight</div>
<div class="learn-tip"><b>Q1: What do the two optimisations do?</b><br/>Path compression flattens the tree during <code>find</code> by pointing every node visited directly at the root. Union by rank keeps trees shallow by always attaching the shorter tree beneath the taller. Individually they give O(log n); together they give O(&alpha;(n)), which is effectively constant.</div>
<div class="learn-tip"><b>Q2: When would you choose DSU over DFS?</b><br/>When edges arrive incrementally and you must answer connectivity queries as you go - DFS would have to re-traverse after every insertion. Also when you only need <i>whether</i> things are connected rather than the actual path. DFS wins when you need paths or the graph is static.</div>
<div class="learn-tip"><b>Q3: How do you check that a graph is a valid tree?</b><br/>Two conditions, both required: exactly <code>n-1</code> edges, and no edge joins two nodes already in the same set. <code>n-1</code> edges alone permits a cycle plus a disconnected node; acyclicity alone permits a forest.</div>
<div class="learn-tip"><b>Q4: Can you undo a union?</b><br/>Not with path compression, because it destroys the original structure. If you need rollback, use union by rank <i>without</i> compression and keep a stack of the changes - that gives O(log n) operations with undo, which is the basis of offline dynamic connectivity.</div>
</div>`,
    code: `// P25 - Union-Find (DSU).
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class DSU {
    vector<int> parent, rank_;
    int components;
public:
    explicit DSU(int n) : parent(n), rank_(n, 0), components(n) {
        iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0);      // each node is its own root
    }

    int find(int x) {                               // PATH COMPRESSION
        if (parent[x] != x) parent[x] = find(parent[x]);
        return parent[x];
    }

    bool unite(int a, int b) {                      // UNION BY RANK
        int ra = find(a), rb = find(b);
        if (ra == rb) return false;                 // already together -> cycle
        if (rank_[ra] < rank_[rb]) swap(ra, rb);
        parent[rb] = ra;                            // union the ROOTS, never raw nodes
        if (rank_[ra] == rank_[rb]) ++rank_[ra];
        --components;
        return true;
    }

    bool connected(int a, int b) { return find(a) == find(b); }
    int count() const { return components; }
};

// ---- 684. Redundant Connection ----
vector<int> findRedundantConnection(vector<vector<int>>& edges) {
    DSU dsu((int)edges.size() + 1);
    for (auto& e : edges)
        if (!dsu.unite(e[0], e[1])) return e;       // first edge closing a cycle
    return {};
}

// ---- 261. Graph Valid Tree: n-1 edges AND acyclic ----
bool validTree(int n, vector<vector<int>>& edges) {
    if ((int)edges.size() != n - 1) return false;   // necessary, not sufficient
    DSU dsu(n);
    for (auto& e : edges)
        if (!dsu.unite(e[0], e[1])) return false;   // a cycle
    return dsu.count() == 1;
}

// ---- 323. Number of Connected Components ----
int countComponents(int n, vector<vector<int>>& edges) {
    DSU dsu(n);
    for (auto& e : edges) dsu.unite(e[0], e[1]);
    return dsu.count();
}

// ---- 1584. Min Cost to Connect All Points: Kruskal's MST ----
int minCostConnectPoints(vector<vector<int>>& pts) {
    int n = (int)pts.size();
    vector<tuple<int,int,int>> edges;               // (weight, u, v)
    edges.reserve((size_t)n * (n - 1) / 2);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i)
        for (int j = i + 1; j < n; ++j)
            edges.push_back({abs(pts[i][0]-pts[j][0]) + abs(pts[i][1]-pts[j][1]), i, j});

    sort(edges.begin(), edges.end());               // cheapest first
    DSU dsu(n);
    int total = 0, used = 0;
    for (auto [w, u, v] : edges) {
        if (dsu.unite(u, v)) {                      // no cycle -> take the edge
            total += w;
            if (++used == n - 1) break;             // a spanning tree is complete
        }
    }
    return total;
}

// ---- 721. Accounts Merge: union by shared attribute ----
vector<vector<string>> accountsMerge(vector<vector<string>>& accounts) {
    unordered_map<string,int> owner;                // email -> account index
    unordered_map<string,string> name;              // email -> person
    DSU dsu((int)accounts.size());

    for (int i = 0; i < (int)accounts.size(); ++i)
        for (size_t j = 1; j < accounts[i].size(); ++j) {
            const string& mail = accounts[i][j];
            name[mail] = accounts[i][0];
            if (owner.count(mail)) dsu.unite(i, owner[mail]);   // seen before -> merge
            else owner[mail] = i;
        }

    map<int, set<string>> grouped;
    for (auto& [mail, idx] : owner) grouped[dsu.find(idx)].insert(mail);

    vector<vector<string>> out;
    for (auto& [root, mails] : grouped) {
        vector<string> row{name[*mails.begin()]};
        row.insert(row.end(), mails.begin(), mails.end());       // set => sorted
        out.push_back(std::move(row));
    }
    return out;
}`,
    problems: DSA_PROBLEMS[25],
    mcqs: [
      { q: 'union must always link:', o: ['The two roots returned by find', 'The smaller-numbered nodes', 'Adjacent nodes only', 'The two raw nodes'], a: 0, e: 'parent[a] = b corrupts the forest; use parent[find(a)] = find(b).' },
      { q: 'Path compression plus union by rank gives amortised:', o: ['O(log n)', 'O(alpha(n)), effectively constant', 'O(n)', 'O(1) worst case'], a: 1, e: 'Inverse Ackermann is under 5 for any realistic n.' },
      { q: 'A graph is a valid tree when:', o: ['It has n-1 edges', 'It is acyclic', 'It has n-1 edges AND is acyclic', 'It is connected only'], a: 2, e: 'Either condition alone allows a counterexample.' },
      { q: 'DSU is preferred over DFS when:', o: ['You need the actual path', 'The graph is a tree', 'The graph is dense', 'Edges arrive incrementally and connectivity is queried as you go'], a: 3, e: 'DFS would have to re-traverse after every insertion.' }
    ]
  }
